  ],
  "text": "Nine to five is a machine's schedule. People were fitted into it about a hundred and fifty years ago. Before factories, most work went by daylight and by task. You worked until the thing was finished. A machine does not care about daylight, and it costs money every minute it is not running. So the work stopped being the task and became the hours. Which meant everyone in the building had to agree what time it actually was. A whistle. A gate. A clock on the wall deciding whether you were late. Railways forced the same thing on whole countries, because a timetable needs one clock. Before that, every town kept its own noon, set by its own sun. And the weekend and the eight hour day are not traditions. They were won, in response to this. The rhythm you live by was designed for equipment, and then handed to you. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
